# Citation metadata for tradefloor, in Citation File Format 1.2.0.
# https://citation-file-format.github.io/
#
# NO DOI YET. A DOI is deliberately absent rather than invented. `.zenodo.json`
# beside this file is ready for Zenodo's GitHub integration, which mints one
# DOI per GitHub release and one concept DOI for the project as a whole. The
# owner switches that integration on (RELEASING.md, "DOI (Zenodo)"). When the
# first DOI exists, add the concept DOI here as `doi:`, and keep
# `.zenodo.json` and this file saying the same thing: Zenodo reads
# `.zenodo.json` first.
#
# `version:` tracks the `version` field in pyproject.toml, and
# `date-released:` is the day that version was tagged. Both move together at
# a release; a version without the date it shipped is half a citation.
#
# A citation identifies the software. It does not identify a model or a RUN.
# Name the preset beside the version (for example pt-v20): results depend on
# it, and the default preset has changed between minor releases. Reproducing
# a published result needs four more things: the seed, the universe
# fingerprint and how it was built, the macro initial conditions, and the
# order log. `RunManifest` records them. The model itself is specified in
# docs/MODEL.md, and docs/SUPPORT.md says which release lines are supported
# and for how long.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata, and name the model preset you used (for example pt-v20)."
title: tradefloor
abstract: >-
  A deterministic market simulator with a real limit order book. A Rust core
  with a Python API. The same seed produces the same market on every platform,
  every price move carries a labelled factor decomposition, and orders match
  against depth so trading moves the price.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Coombes
    given-names: Simon
    email: simon@coombes.io
version: 0.8.5
date-released: "2026-09-24"
license:
  - MIT
  - Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/simoncoombes/tradefloor"
url: "https://tradefloor.dev/"
keywords:
  - market simulation
  - limit order book
  - deterministic simulation
  - agent-based finance
  - reproducible research
  - transaction cost analysis
